Left over from k338 (2026-10-31), where the registry.local writer and its drift probe now read the Corefile through one parser (OCP::Corefile):
- k3s `coredns-custom` ConfigMap `*.server` snippets: CoreDNS on k3s imports server blocks from that second ConfigMap. Neither the writer nor the probe looks at it, so a snippet that answers registry.local (or shadows it) is invisible to both. ADR 0022 treats this as solved; it is not. Decide: read coredns-custom on k3s too (and through OCP::Corefile), or say in the ADR that it is out of scope.
- A CoreDNS ConfigMap with no `Corefile` key: the writer dies (k328, a [WARN] on STDERR), the probe moves on or reports nothing. They still disagree there.
- The writer repairs the duplicate-hosts Corefile that crashes CoreDNS; the probe does not detect it.
- Server-block keys with a port are matched by zone only (as before k338) -- fine for :53, say so if another port appears.

Done = writer and probe agree on each case (t/338 extended), or an ADR line saying which case is deliberately not handled.

